The approach uses a single-position rotation between two high-beta leveraged equity ETFs, allocating to whichever one shows the strongest recent momentum after adjusting for risk. If neither qualifies, it shifts entirely into short-term Treasury exposure for safety. Each day, it calculates a 21-day momentum measure for TQQQ (leveraged Nasdaq 100) and ERX (leveraged U.S. energy stocks), then divides that return by the asset’s recent daily volatility to produce a risk-adjusted momentum score. The portfolio holds only the top-ranked asset when its score is positive; if both scores fall below zero, it moves fully into BIL to preserve capital. The goal is to capture short-term trends while reducing the impact of sudden regime changes by normalizing for risk.

Rebalancing happens once per day near market close using daily data, with intraday minute data used strictly for trade execution. A hysteresis rule limits excessive switching: the strategy only rotates into a new asset if its score exceeds the current holding’s score by at least 0.05, and it stays out of risk assets until a positive signal returns. Position sizing follows a volatility-targeting framework: the selected asset’s weight is set to 40% divided by its annualized 20-day realized volatility, capped at 100%, with any leftover allocated to BIL. Adjustments are made only when weights drift by more than 5%, and any non-target holdings are fully liquidated.